#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I
"""rea-sandbox: run REA (rea-agents) for tm8 inside gVisor, scoped per teammate.

Every REA process runs in a fresh container: gVisor (runsc), no network, read-only
root, all capabilities dropped, capped CPU / memory / pids, the case's targets
read-only and only the case's out/ and work/ writable. Engines are mounted
read-only from <lab>/engines; licensed engines only once their slot is READY.

  rea-sandbox call <tool> [json]   one REA MCP tool call in this session's warm REA
  rea-sandbox tools [<tool>...]    the tools this role may call; with names, their schemas
  rea-sandbox stop                 end this session's warm REAs now (with --case, only that case's)
  rea-sandbox mcp                  stdio MCP server for an MCP client
  rea-sandbox run -- <rea args>    one REA CLI command; prints REA's output
  rea-sandbox doctor [provider]    `rea doctor --json` with this session's engines
  rea-sandbox engines              every engine slot: missing / present / ready
  rea-sandbox intake <src>...      copy files, dirs or https URLs into the case
  rea-sandbox scope                role, families, tools, engines, case, limits
  rea-sandbox jeb <target>         start JEB's MCP server in this session's container
  rea-sandbox setup <slot>         install the owner's IDA, JEB or Hopper, then smoke-test it
  rea-sandbox ps                   running sandbox containers

Scope comes from TM8_TEAM_MEMBER_ID (config "members" -> role), the case from
TM8_TASK_IDS (first id) or TM8_SESSION_ID. REA 6.8.0 cannot filter tool families
itself, so `mcp` and `call` filter tools/list and refuse out-of-scope tools/call here.
Roles route work between teammates; they are not a security boundary (every session
runs as the same host user). The boundary is the sandbox around REA and its targets.

Profiles keep licences away from target code. `static` (the default) mounts every
ready engine, including the owner's licensed IDA, Hopper and JEB, and refuses tools
that run the target. `exec` is for running it (process capture, GDB, Rizin debug,
browser): no licensed or heavy engines, plus an executable tmpfs at /exec (install a
target there first; /tmp is noexec). `run` passes REA's CLI through unfiltered, so it
never mounts a licensed engine.
"""

